  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Women Thrive in Bangladesh Activity</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Bangladesh):  While Bangladesh&apos;s garment industry has largely recovered, high post-pandemic inflation has severely impacted garment factory workers, worsening inequalities, especially for women in the ready-made garments (RMG) sector. Although the RMG sector is the second-largest economic sector in Bangladesh, studies show a continuing decline in female RMG workers, who now make up 54 percent of the workforce. With FY 2024 funds, the W-GDP Women Thrive in Bangladesh (Thrive) activity will support over 100,000 female RMG workers in developing professional and leadership skills, mainly within PVH Corporation supply chain factories and surrounding communities. The White House Women&apos;s Global Development and Prosperity initiative has allocated $3 million for the project, with USAID/Bangladesh providing additional funds. This initiative aims to reduce the gender gap in RMG factories and strengthen women&apos;s advocacy and negotiation skills, advancing their rights and leadership. Thrive will operate in Dhaka, Gazipur, Mymensingh, Kishoreganj, Chattogram, and Narayanganj. The activity will enhance the livelihoods and well-being of workers, particularly female workers, by increasing efficiency and job retention. It will create career progression opportunities for women by fostering employer buy-in and building a more resilient female workforce. Additionally, it will connect women to other employment opportunities, helping to mitigate the impacts of the COVID-19 pandemic and other future challenges.This IM contributes to the Women&apos;s Leadership earmark ($200k) and Private Sector Engagement ($200k). It is in line with the Integrated Country Strategy Mission Goal 2 - Bangladesh is committed to democracy, transparency, pluralism, tolerance, good governance, and respect for human rights, and supports USAID&apos;s CDCS Mission Development Objective 1 - Democracy Strengthened for an Inclusive Society.</narrative>
  </description>
